WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.435
-Our next guest is
a very funny comedian,

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.604
actor and author,
you can see him on the road

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.805
touring around the country.

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:07.240
For ticket information
go to colinquinn.com.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.408
Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:10.810
our very good friend
Colin Quinn everybody.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.045
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:23.456 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.058
Welcome back.

00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:26.692
You are one of our modern
historians

00:00:26.692 --> 00:00:28.328
-Whoa!
-...of this great city.

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:29.396
-Yes.

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:30.396
-You know a lot
about New York City.

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:31.430
And you have a great new...

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:32.665
-You're coming in hot,
by the way.

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:34.367
-I'm coming in hot.
I want to big you up.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.002
-You've been on already.
You've been on already.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.003
I just got out here.

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:38.004
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:39.238
And by the way,
before we start

00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:41.874
not to break the momentum,

00:00:41.874 --> 00:00:43.609
I don't like this.
-Okay.

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:45.345
-The old days of a nice cup.
-Yeah.

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:47.180
-Look at this.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. Sorry.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.981
-Jesus.
-You really?

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:50.916
-Because it makes me
look cheap now

00:00:50.916 --> 00:00:51.918
that I'm drinking it.

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:52.919
[ Laughter ]

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:53.953
The crowd doesn't know.

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:55.187
They'll be like,
Look at that chiseler.

00:00:55.187 --> 00:00:57.790
People on TV,
it's all about perception.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.225
You know what I mean,
folks?

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.893
[ Laughter ]

00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:02.928
I like to work the crowd.
Work in comedy is big now.

00:01:02.928 --> 00:01:05.097
So I try to work
the crowd on TV.

00:01:05.097 --> 00:01:07.901
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:08.901
-Oh, great.

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:10.503
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:11.837
-Oh.
-That should be --

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:14.107
That should be your new
catchphrase as a comedian.

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.575
It isn't like
an actual catchphrase,

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:16.743
but after your punch lines,

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.711
you take a sip
from a tiny thing of water.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:19.979
[ Laughter ]

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.114
It gave them the exact right
amount of time to laugh.

00:01:22.114 --> 00:01:23.349
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:24.884
-It might look a little
creepy, but you're right.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:26.252
-Just tiny sips.
-Yeah.

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:27.486
And the whole thing
is, he makes it --

00:01:27.486 --> 00:01:28.855
You know,
he makes it last the whole hour.

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:29.855
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:31.925
Tiny little sips.
You have a --

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:33.926
I was -- I was setting you up.
Yes, I know.

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:35.194
I know.
You were talk--

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:36.229
You have
this new show called --

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:37.263
-And you're like, "Hey."
-Hey.

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:38.364
Look, you want
to talk about

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:39.865
"Block by Block,"
no problem.

00:01:39.865 --> 00:01:41.267
You have a YouTube
show called "Block by Block"

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.102
where you're going
to different neighborhoods

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:44.203
in New York City.
-Yeah.

00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:45.704
-And you're talking
about the history.

00:01:45.704 --> 00:01:46.972
What makes them unique.
-Yes.

00:01:46.972 --> 00:01:48.007
You've done Yorkville.

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:49.575
You've done Hell's Kitchen.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:51.744
When you were growing up
in New York,

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.946
did different neighborhoods
have different reputations

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:55.148
that were interesting to you?

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:57.616
-Oh, sure.
But you can't discuss those now.

00:01:57.616 --> 00:01:59.118
They're all based on ethnicity.

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:01.921
-Okay, gotcha. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:03.289
So now you just are like,
"Oh, that --

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:04.691
Oh, I know that one.
That's northeast of here.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:05.992
-You're like,
Oh, that block, yeah.

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:07.660
[ Laughter ]

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.995
You have to get
very vague.

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:11.030
But, um, yeah.
But these guys, you know,

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.231
these people
from back in the day.

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:14.800
So my friend Steve Kelly
in Yorkville

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.569
and he goes over the history,

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:18.171
but also the brawls --
the giant fights

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.406
they'd have at
the Second Avenue bars.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:23.676
He left his comic strip post
to go across the street,

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:25.677
got in a big brawl.
The cops are everywhere.

00:02:25.677 --> 00:02:27.379
And he goes back
to the comic strip

00:02:27.379 --> 00:02:30.016
where, ironically,
he's in charge of security.

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:32.184
And then my friend Mike Spillane

00:02:32.184 --> 00:02:33.620
was was telling the story
about him

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.654
and his brother and his cousin
and a couple of other guys.

00:02:35.654 --> 00:02:38.490
And they get in a fight
in a bar, a couple of guys,

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.193
but then he realize
one of the guy's friends

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:41.861
they're fighting
is Andre the Giant.

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:43.496
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.632
So he picks him up
and just flings him.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.033
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.734
-I feel like you should get
a visual

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:50.703
on Andre the Giant
before the fight starts.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:51.737
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.

00:02:51.737 --> 00:02:52.871
No, actually,
he did see him

00:02:52.871 --> 00:02:54.307
in the bathroom
and he goes, "Oh, wow."

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:55.608
But he didn't know he
was with the guy...

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.076
-Oh, that's the problem.
-...boys were about the fight.

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:58.645
-I think just out of safety,
if Andre the Giant was there,

00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:00.513
I wouldn't start any fights.

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.381
You never know
who's his plus one.

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:03.715
Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.715 --> 00:03:06.519
-Yes. Andre the Giant
with a plus one. I like that.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:07.853
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:09.655
-And did you -- What were, like,
what were sort of like --

00:03:09.655 --> 00:03:10.789
What about Flushing?

00:03:10.789 --> 00:03:12.525
What was the reputation
about Flushing.

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.627
-Flushing, back then
it was kind of Irish, Italian,

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.429
Jewish, like a lot of names,
and now, it's all Asian.

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:19.432
And then it became Korean
and then it became Chinese.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:21.533
So even the Koreans
now have left Flushing.

00:03:21.533 --> 00:03:23.202
They moved to Bayside.

00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:24.904
-So it's just like it
just rotates through.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.505
You just have like one
after the other,

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:27.774
a different identity comes
through.

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:29.409
-Yeah.
-That's fascinating.

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:30.877
-I agree.

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:33.779
-You talked about small talk
and how it's a lost art,

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:35.181
but I was wondering,

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:36.883
small talk
in different countries,

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:38.551
you travel a lot,
do you find like

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.920
certain cultures
are better at small talk?

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.422
-Well, I mean Irish.

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:44.323
It depends what you like
for small talk,

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:47.626
if you like being -- having,
you know, your balls busted,

00:03:47.626 --> 00:03:49.061
The Irish people.

00:03:49.061 --> 00:03:50.062
-Yeah.
-You know.

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:51.263
They like that banter.

00:03:51.263 --> 00:03:52.331
-Yeah.
-Is what I mean.

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:53.365
You know what I mean?

00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:55.735
So I was in Ireland,
like we went,

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:57.503
we went last summer
to Ireland.

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:59.738
So I go to the -- There's
a girl behind this, like,

00:03:59.738 --> 00:04:01.474
podium at the hotel restaurant.

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:03.543
So I'm the guy that likes to,
you know,

00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:06.112
like, I like to play around
with all the people,

00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:08.514
Like I'm the guy in my building
if somebody's mopping,

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:10.549
every time
I walk in the lobby,

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:12.585
I pretend to slip like,
"Hey!" and pretend.

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:13.586
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:14.921
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:16.822
-It's not the Mark Twain
humor prize,

00:04:16.822 --> 00:04:18.491
but, you know, that's...

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:19.525
That's my thing.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:20.727
I like to be like,
"Hey!"

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:22.295
Have a couple
of laughs and move on.

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:24.430
Nobody gets hurt.
We'll, have a nice laugh.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.165
And, um...

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:27.967
I'm getting mad thinking about
people judging.

00:04:27.967 --> 00:04:28.968
And...

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:31.470
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.806
Like, "Oh, yeah,
it's funny."

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.775
You know, I'm not --
I'm not a prideful guy, but --

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.443
-Everybody thinks it's funny
that you do that.

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:38.945
I'm sure the guy
mopping must love it.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.813
-He loves it.
-Yeah.

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:41.981
-Every time.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:43.416
And sometimes, like,
if it's the same guy,

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:44.783
then I'll wait
the extra second

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:46.853
because, you know, surprise
is the element in comedy.

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:48.153
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.153 --> 00:04:50.256
And then he's like,
"Oh, he's not going to do it."

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:51.691
And I pass him,
then I do it anyway.

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:53.025
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:54.293
But anyway.

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:55.394
-Ireland.
-Audience will want --

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:56.395
Oh, so I go to Ireland.

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:57.864
So there's a girl
behind the counter

00:04:57.864 --> 00:05:00.132
at the podium at this
restaurant in the hotel,

00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:01.834
and she's quiet, like 19.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.168
But Irish people,
they all have the same

00:05:03.168 --> 00:05:05.203
ballbuster sense of humor.

00:05:05.203 --> 00:05:08.007
So I go, "I want the best table
in here," you know?

00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:09.575
And she goes --
She doesn't know me --

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.177
She goes,
"You deserve no less."

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:12.545
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.545 --> 00:05:14.747
And I go,
"My wife is joining me."

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:16.449
She goes,
"She's a lucky woman."

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:19.017
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.017 --> 00:05:20.019
I love it.

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:21.553
-You -- you
traveled to Greece.

00:05:21.553 --> 00:05:22.888
You were in Greece this summer.

00:05:22.888 --> 00:05:24.156
-I was in Greece
this summer.

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:25.591
-What is your takeaway?
-Epiphany.

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:27.126
I don't think there's --
Maybe that's a Greek word.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:29.195
-I think it is.
-Epiphany.

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:31.430
Well, because they go,
"Do you want to see

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:34.300
the birthplace of democracy
where it all began?"

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.768
And I go, "Yeah."

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:38.070
It's rubble. It's ruins.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.239
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:41.407
So that's what
we're headed for.

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:44.376
We -- Democracy
worked for like 150 years.

00:05:44.376 --> 00:05:46.245
And then it was monarchy,

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:48.247
a great system
for thousands of years.

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:51.851
And then we decided to unearth
this failed rubble system.

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:52.851
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.851 --> 00:05:54.086
You know?
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:56.388
-We had the perfect
setup in 1775,

00:05:56.388 --> 00:05:57.523
a remote king.

00:05:57.523 --> 00:05:58.558
That's the best
form of government.

00:05:58.558 --> 00:05:59.558
-Yeah.

00:05:59.558 --> 00:06:00.626
-He didn't know
what we're doing.

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:01.627
We did whatever
we wanted over here.

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:03.062
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:04.864
But he couldn't -- It'd take
him 40 days to get over here.

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:08.233
But we could use his name.
We'd use his name.

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:09.936
If Spain
and Portugal step to us,

00:06:09.936 --> 00:06:12.504
we're like, "King George
might not like that."

00:06:12.504 --> 00:06:14.774
We gave it all away
for stamps and tea.

00:06:14.774 --> 00:06:16.576
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.576 --> 00:06:17.843
When's the last time
anyone used...?

00:06:17.843 --> 00:06:19.245
It's e-mail
and coffee over here.

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:20.546
You know.

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.414
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:24.249
-You feel like humor
has changed from --

00:06:24.249 --> 00:06:27.052
from when you were growing up,
like the tenor of humor?

00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:28.320
Well, when we
were little kids,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.322
I mean, I remember
as a little kid,

00:06:30.322 --> 00:06:32.725
that's why when people say
you got to make everybody laugh,

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:34.760
you don't have to make
young kids laugh.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.262
I don't want to make
young kids laugh.

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:38.630
I don't have the same sense
of you because I know how dumb

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:40.366
what I thought was funny
when I was young.

00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:44.236
I remember some kid was like,
"Hey, that guy's a real master."

00:06:44.236 --> 00:06:47.439
And he goes, "bater!"
And I was like, at the time.

00:06:47.439 --> 00:06:48.974
I go, That's pretty.
That's pretty good.

00:06:48.974 --> 00:06:50.142
Yeah, yeah.
He put...

00:06:50.142 --> 00:06:51.210
[ Laughter ]

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.479
That he put them
together like that.

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:56.682
I'll give you a test.
Seth spell "I-cup."

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:59.285
-I C-U-P.
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:59.285 --> 00:07:00.486
[ Laughter ]

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:01.487
[ Drum roll ]

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:03.689
[ Laughter ]

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:05.491
-That's the kind of stuff.
-Yeah, So you're saying that --

00:07:05.491 --> 00:07:06.592
-We get when going back.
-In those days,

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.093
-You're not judging them
for enjoying that,

00:07:08.093 --> 00:07:09.261
but you don't want to --
You don't want

00:07:09.261 --> 00:07:10.362
to cater to that audience?

00:07:10.362 --> 00:07:12.031
-No, exactly.

00:07:12.031 --> 00:07:13.699
-And do you feel like comedy

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:16.435
has become more negative
than when you were a kid?

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:18.370
-Well, not just comedy.
Everything's more negative.

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:20.472
I was watching.
Look at Taylor Swift.

00:07:20.472 --> 00:07:21.473
She goes out.

00:07:21.473 --> 00:07:22.541
[ Audience cheers ]

00:07:22.541 --> 00:07:24.343
Right?
She goes to the game.

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:25.577
Like in the old days,
you're like,

00:07:25.577 --> 00:07:26.979
"Oh, look, an athlete
and a singer going out.

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:28.780
This is great."
Everybody's like, "Oh!"

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.284
You know, we don't even
know why everybody's upset,

00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:32.617
But they're like,
I don't like it.

00:07:32.617 --> 00:07:35.320
We're all waiting for the whole
thing to crash and burn.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:36.855
-Yeah.
Every game you're like,

00:07:36.855 --> 00:07:39.158
"Look at her in the stands
with the mother."

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:41.059
It's the most whole--
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:41.059 --> 00:07:44.964
-it's the most wholesome thing
I've ever seen in my life.

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:47.900
-I'm also shocked that
sports fans are upset

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.635
that there's not
more focus on football.

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:52.771
There's so little football
in a football game,

00:07:52.771 --> 00:07:54.573
and it's just those
two guys in a booth.

00:07:54.573 --> 00:07:56.075
They're trying
to make small talk.

00:07:56.075 --> 00:07:57.343
They're trying to make --
-Yes!

00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:59.412
-They got nothing to say.
-That's right.

00:07:59.412 --> 00:08:01.113
-I think it's such
a great relief

00:08:01.113 --> 00:08:03.215
just to cut to people
who seem happy.

00:08:03.215 --> 00:08:05.350
-They must love it.
Yes, they must love it.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:06.752
-Yeah.
-As a Jets fan, were you --

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.153
How do you feel
about the season so far?

00:08:08.153 --> 00:08:09.655
-Well, we're excited because,

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:11.524
first of all,
people don't understand.

00:08:11.524 --> 00:08:13.325
I've been a Jets fan
since I was a little kid.

00:08:13.325 --> 00:08:15.428
The Jets, I was there
when they, well, not there,

00:08:15.428 --> 00:08:17.329
but I was watching or listening.

00:08:17.329 --> 00:08:18.530
I forget how it
was in those days.

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:20.565
But when the Jets,
when Joe Namath was there,

00:08:20.565 --> 00:08:22.467
and I remember thinking
even as a 10-year-old kid

00:08:22.467 --> 00:08:23.736
like, "This makes sense."

00:08:23.736 --> 00:08:25.338
It's New York,
it's a big market.

00:08:25.338 --> 00:08:27.840
It'll probably be
like this forever, right?

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.509
Little did I know.

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:32.077
But when Aaron Rodgers
got the Achilles,

00:08:32.077 --> 00:08:35.080
the first set of downs,
any real Jets fan was not like,

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:36.782
"I can't believe it."
Of course.

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:38.050
What else would you
expect to happen?

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:39.051
Right.

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:40.186
[ Laughter ]

00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:41.553
-So you weren't
that surprised?

00:08:41.553 --> 00:08:45.824
-No, I wasn't at all.
I mean, it made perfect sense.

00:08:45.824 --> 00:08:47.292
-We're fellow writers.
Writer strike's over.

00:08:47.292 --> 00:08:48.361
That's a good thing.

00:08:48.361 --> 00:08:50.295
You, um, you have
a negotiating tactic,

00:08:50.295 --> 00:08:51.430
because I know a lot
of people were --

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:52.531
had their notes about like,

00:08:52.531 --> 00:08:53.832
"Oh, if I was on
the negotiating committee,

00:08:53.832 --> 00:08:55.101
I would have say this or that."

00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:57.303
But you -- you take care
of your own contract.

00:08:57.303 --> 00:09:00.205
-Two months before
the writer's strike,

00:09:00.205 --> 00:09:01.440
I happen to be telling my wife,

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.142
who, you know,
deals with that kind of stuff,

00:09:03.142 --> 00:09:05.010
I go when I --
All my career,

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:07.546
a little fun secret fact
I thought about me,

00:09:07.546 --> 00:09:09.949
I go all my whole career
whenever they hand me

00:09:09.949 --> 00:09:11.650
the contract,
this started in the '80s,

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:14.854
and it says in perpetuity
in all forms existing

00:09:14.854 --> 00:09:15.954
and in the future.

00:09:15.954 --> 00:09:17.156
Back in the '80s,

00:09:17.156 --> 00:09:19.025
this one is one of
the big contract points,

00:09:19.025 --> 00:09:22.061
but I always cross it off
in every contract

00:09:22.061 --> 00:09:23.795
anyone ever handed me,
if it was on the show.

00:09:23.795 --> 00:09:25.931
Anyway, I cross it off
with my pen.

00:09:25.931 --> 00:09:27.366
So I told her that

00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:30.369
and she literally spent
a minute laughing in my face.

00:09:30.369 --> 00:09:31.670
[ Laughter ]

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:33.872
-So in perpetuity, you were
just never comfortable

00:09:33.872 --> 00:09:35.941
with people
owning it forever.

00:09:35.941 --> 00:09:38.110
-Yeah, in all forms,
existing and in the future.

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:39.478
You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:09:39.478 --> 00:09:41.279
-So what would you -- what
would you negotiate instead?

00:09:41.279 --> 00:09:44.750
100 years?

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:46.485
Well, just --
I don't like these...

00:09:46.485 --> 00:09:48.387
Well, who knew?
This is before any --

00:09:48.387 --> 00:09:50.722
This is before the streaming,
Anything like that.

00:09:50.722 --> 00:09:52.024
You know, AI.

00:09:52.024 --> 00:09:53.592
I was ahead of the game
is what I'm trying to say.

00:09:53.592 --> 00:09:55.094
-Yeah.

00:09:55.094 --> 00:09:56.495
And she laughs at you.
You're ahead of the game.

00:09:56.495 --> 00:09:58.630
-She laughed at me.
-A futurist like yourself?

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:00.399
-Yeah, exactly.
-[ Laughs ]

00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:03.002
-Exactly.
-One final thing.

00:10:03.002 --> 00:10:06.171
You know, obviously you interact
with showbiz people.

00:10:06.171 --> 00:10:07.539
There's a lot of showbiz
small talk.

00:10:07.539 --> 00:10:08.874
Sometimes people will say,

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:10.276
"We should get together
sometime."

00:10:10.276 --> 00:10:11.777
-Yeah.
Are you the kind of person

00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:13.612
who likes to make plans
like that?

00:10:13.612 --> 00:10:14.914
-Well, I don't.
I hate it.

00:10:14.914 --> 00:10:17.717
but I always say,
"Yes, absolutely."

00:10:17.717 --> 00:10:19.217
-Okay.
So that would be your advice.

00:10:19.217 --> 00:10:20.486
-Never show up.

00:10:20.486 --> 00:10:22.421
And then some people
actually have the nerve to,

00:10:22.421 --> 00:10:23.722
like, call you on like,

00:10:23.722 --> 00:10:24.856
"Hey, I thought we're getting
together for lunch."

00:10:24.856 --> 00:10:26.926
I was like, "Um,
what are you talking about?"

00:10:26.926 --> 00:10:28.393
[ Laughter ]

00:10:28.393 --> 00:10:29.528
Did you seriously believe

00:10:29.528 --> 00:10:30.930
we were going to get
together for lunch?

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.532
[ Laughter ]

00:10:33.532 --> 00:10:34.967
So weird to me,
you know,

00:10:34.967 --> 00:10:36.668
-So -- Right.
So it seems you --

00:10:36.668 --> 00:10:37.903
It's just like words.

00:10:37.903 --> 00:10:39.138
Like if someone's like,
"We should get together."

00:10:39.138 --> 00:10:40.572
And you're like,
"Yeah, absolutely."

00:10:40.572 --> 00:10:42.107
That's just like a pleasant way
of ending a conversation.

00:10:42.107 --> 00:10:43.542
-Well, if I say it,

00:10:43.542 --> 00:10:44.877
then I'm insulted
if they don't want to do it.

00:10:44.877 --> 00:10:46.145
-Sure, sure.

00:10:46.145 --> 00:10:47.346
That's a totally different --

00:10:47.346 --> 00:10:48.414
-That's a different dynamic.

00:10:48.414 --> 00:10:49.515
-Hey, thanks so much
for being here.

00:10:49.515 --> 00:10:50.516
It's always a pleasure
to talk to you,

00:10:50.516 --> 00:10:51.717
Colin Quinn.

00:10:51.717 --> 00:10:53.619
For ticket information
go to colinquinn.com.

